A||N||9||William Federle||4 cylinder head removal||||5
Z||000000||William Federle||10-23-1999||08:15 AM||||I was removing my spark plugs to gap them<BR>in my 1984 SE and the #1 sparkplug broke<BR>off. The hex came out with the insulator<BR>and the thread stayed in the head. I've<BR>tried an easy-out but can't move it. It <BR>looks like I'll have to drill it out and <BR>use an insert. I've checked the "online <BR>service manual" but head removal isn't one<BR>of the topics. Has this happened to anyone<BR>else and if so is there some secret to getting the plug thread out? I just got this<BR>car for a very low price but I'd like to get<BR>past this problem and keep it. It's a lot of<BR>fun to drive.||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||10-23-1999||09:31 AM||||Bummer man. It's going to be a real "b!tch" to drill out and retap it without getting metal shavings down into the cylinder (which is very bad). I know it's going to be a big headache, but you probably should pull the head off. If you do, you might as well take it into the machine shop and have them rebuild it and fix the spark plug threads too. At least have the valves ground and new seals put in.<P>I didn't know that this Forum would not allow certain words (like the "b" word)! Oh well, there are ways to get around most everything.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 10-23-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Dan||10-23-1999||09:52 AM||||I had an Ac plug do that. What brand of plug was it?||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-23-1999||12:13 PM||||Removing the head is farely easy. you have to take off the EGR valve, valve cover, and exhaust manifold. You need the top alternator bolt out, and the dog bone off. you can leave everthing on the intake. disconnect the intake and tie it off. tie it to a board across the boddy so its supported.<P>lossen the rockers and take out the push rods. KEEP THE PUSH RODS IN ORDER!<P><BR>you'll need the head off for this method.... but it avoids having to use inserts in cast heads.<P>You need a carbide rod blade for a hack saw. a dremmel tool with a long thin stone works too. I haven't seen a carbide rod in a while. I think they should fit thru the thin body plugs.<P>break off the electrode and thread the rod tru the plug. mount the rod in the saw frame. pull it tight, use a good saw frame. a cheap frame won't keep the rod tight enough.<P>slot the plug body in at least 2 places untill the threads start to show. This will releave the plug pressure against the threads. For a bit more releaf take a flat blade screw driver, or better a small chisle, and place it on the end of the plug.<P>if you knick the threads in the head a tiny bit it's ok. as long as the rest of the threads are in decent shape. after you get the plug out use a thread chaser and clean all the holes out good.<P>durring reassembly stuff the holes with rag to keep out loose crap while you work.<P>install the new plugs with a bit if anti sieze on them. DO NOT over tighten the plugs! This is very easy to do with the anti sieze on.<P><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-23-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||William Federle||10-23-1999||06:50 PM||||I want to thank you guys who replied with<BR>thoughtful suggestions. I finally got this<BR>thing fixed this afternoon. I used the easy out that I had bought yesterday (a Hanson EX5 screw extractor) and a 1/2 " drive 3"<BR>extension with a craftsman 1/2-3/8 adapter<BR>on the end that engaged the extractor. I used<BR>a 24" cresent wrench on the 1/2 inch drive. <BR>It had soaked overnite with penetrating oil.<BR>Whatever the reason, the extra muscle I was<BR>able to apply with the big wrench paid off. I then spent a couple of hours with a magnetic screwdriver cleaning up as much of the stuff that was sitting on top of the piston as I could see with a mirror and a flashlight and then put in new sparkplugs with anti-sieze compound and a torque wrench - 15 ft lbs. Once again, thanks to everyone who replied.<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000005||William Federle||10-23-1999||06:55 PM||||I'm sorry - I forgot - the plug that broke<BR>off was a Champion. I think whoever pulled it<BR>in there just put it in too tight and didn't<BR>use anti-sieze.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||10-24-1999||01:19 AM||||Good deal William! Sounds like you lucked out!||||reg||1
Z||000007||William Federle||10-24-1999||08:02 AM||||Yes, I really dodged a bullet this time<BR>Batboy - but I printed out this thread <BR>anyway because sooner or later I might have<BR>to remove the head anyway. I'm just glad <BR>that I didn't have to do it now.||||reg||12
Z||000008||88formula||10-25-1999||08:11 PM||||Be carefull not to get the anti-seize on the insulator of the spark plug as this will cause the engine to misfire. Anti-seize coducts electricity very well. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||William Federle||10-25-1999||08:22 PM||||I didn't know that anti-sieze was conductive<BR>but I did strive to keep it just on the threads. I've driven it 50 miles to work and<BR>50 miles back since then and it didn't seem<BR>to misfire. I guess I lucked out again. Thanks for your reply. ||||reg||14
